          "update_time": 1793049730.569,
          "content": {
            "content_type": "text",
            "parts": [
              "The key difference is that Rodgers–Tao’s assumption gives the zeta zeros time to relax before reaching \\(t=0\\), where their spacing can be compared with known results. Under \\(\\Lambda>0\\), the real-zero flow begins after \\(t=0\\); regular spacing later is compatible with that assumption. I’m developing the useful alternative: what estimates could exclude a collision at a positive threshold?\n"
            ]
          },
